Well, there are actually about four different save file formats, but for
the most part they're the same.

The code Ken refers to handles the way that NetHack saves the
list of objects in the save file. The norm is to save the whole objects
array which includes the strings describing the real contents of the
object (i.e. a potion of healing, gain level, etc.). Under Unix, the
addreses of these strings will remain constant since each process
gets its own address space (right?) while on the Mac(especially with
MultiFinder), several programs share the same address space. This
code also fixes some other port-specific differences.

Thus, without the extra code, if you tried to restore your character
and performed an inventory, the strings may be even more incomprehensible
than before unless NetHack loaded in EXACTLY the same location as before.
(Try the Mac port of Nethack 2.3 for an example of this)

The solution I hit upon was to have an identifier for each object in
the array and just before saving the array, place these identifiers
where the string addresses occurred. On restoration, the identifiers
are matched with the correct object and the current addresses for
the description strings are stored in the objects array.

There is at least one other difference between save files not mentioned
before.
The type of compression used depends on the OS. On Unix, Compress or
no compression is used, VMS uses none right now(I think). There is an
option for run-length encoding of zeros since the dungeon map contains
many zeros. I believe some of the microcomputer ports use this, while
the Mac port uses built-in run-length encoding routines.

As mentioned before, it also depends on whether your CPU is little-endian
or big-endian.
So that's why you can't use a save file from one port on another.

